# Overlay: extend the encoder's acoustic context window from ~2 s to ~4 s.
#
# Load this AFTER whichever guitar_pilot config you are already using, so its
# TASK_FEATURE_LENGTHS rebinding wins:
#
#   --gin_file=mt3/gin/model.gin
#   --gin_file=mt3/gin/train.gin
#   --gin_file=mt3/gin/guitar_pilot_finetune_modal.gin
#   --gin_file=mt3/gin/context_4s.gin
#
# It is deliberately a thin overlay rather than a forked copy of the finetune
# config: the 2 s path must stay byte-identical so that a 2 s-vs-4 s ablation
# differs in exactly one causal factor (whitepaper 4.3, "change one causal
# factor at a time").
#
# WINDOW ARITHMETIC. The window length is never written in seconds anywhere in
# this codebase -- it is derived from TASK_FEATURE_LENGTHS['inputs'], which
# counts spectrogram FRAMES. spectrograms.py fixes DEFAULT_SAMPLE_RATE=16000
# and DEFAULT_HOP_WIDTH=128, so frames_per_second = 16000/128 = 125:
#
#   256 frames / 125 fps = 2.048 s   (baseline)
#   512 frames / 125 fps = 4.096 s   (this file)
#
# Nothing downstream needs to be told about the change. The training task's
# t5.data.preprocessors.select_random_chunk and the eval task's
# split_tokens_to_inputs_length both read sequence_length['inputs'] at
# pipeline-construction time (mt3/tasks.py), so the new value propagates to
# both chunking paths automatically.
#
# CHECKPOINT COMPATIBILITY. This is a pure sequence-length change and needs no
# checkpoint surgery. t5x's T5Config carries no sequence-length-dependent
# field, and the encoder builds its position signal with
# layers.RelativePositionBiases(num_buckets=32, max_distance=128), called as
# relative_embedding(inputs.shape[-2], inputs.shape[-2], ...) -- i.e. the
# length is read from the live input and looked up in a bucket table shaped
# [num_buckets, num_heads], independent of sequence length. Every tensor in
# checkpoint_0 therefore keeps its shape and restores directly. (The whitepaper
# flags "positional migration" as the main compatibility risk of Initiative 4;
# that warning assumes ABSOLUTE position embeddings and does not apply here.)
#
# The behavioural caveat does still apply: max_distance=128 means every pair of
# frames more than 128 apart (~1.02 s) already shares the single coarsest
# bucket. At 512 frames a much larger fraction of each window sits inside that
# saturated bucket than the pretrained model ever had to resolve, so expect to
# need adaptation steps -- the weights load, but the model has not learned to
# USE the extra context yet.
#
# TARGETS IS RAISED TO 2048, AND THAT IS NOT OPTIONAL. Measured with
# scripts/measure_target_lengths.py on guitar_pilot_notes_ties_vb1_train:
#
#   window          median   p95    p99    max     vs 1024
#   256 (~2.048 s)     112   323    435    608     fits
#   256, other pass    118   295    501    911     fits (thin)
#   512 (~4.096 s)     187   571    811   1249     fits
#   512, other pass    185   518    893   1533     OVERFLOWS (2/500 = 0.4%)
#
# Leaving targets at 1024 does not degrade gracefully: tasks.py ends the
# training pipeline with handle_too_long(skip=skip_too_long) and skip_too_long
# defaults to False, which is the assert_not_too_long branch -- a raise, not a
# filter. A 0.4% per-window overflow rate is a near-certain crash over a real
# run, at an unpredictable step, on a paid GPU.
#
# 2048 gives ~33% headroom over the highest length observed anywhere (1533).
# It cannot be called a proven bound: select_random_chunk draws a different
# random chunk from each cached segment every epoch, so two full-epoch passes
# returned maxima of 1249 and 1533. No single pass bounds the tail; re-run the
# script if the corpus changes.
#
# COST. seqio pads every feature to its task_feature_length (models.py:
# "Each feature in the task_feature_lengths is trimmed/padded") because XLA
# needs static shapes, so the decoder computes at the FULL target length every
# step regardless of actual content -- with a median of 187, almost all of that
# work is padding. Going 1024 -> 2048 therefore quadruples decoder
# self-attention cost on top of the ~4x encoder attention from the window
# itself. Both are unconditional. Budget for it before launching.

from __gin__ import dynamic_registration

TASK_FEATURE_LENGTHS = {'inputs': 512, 'targets': 2048}
